---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Analog Synthesizer">

  <section class="prose max-w-none">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Analog Synthesizer</h1>

    <!-- Project Description -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Project Description</h2>

    <p>
      This project is a fully analog synthesizer with a digital waveform display.
      The audio signal path is constructed from discrete components, op-amps, and
      operational transconductance amplifiers (OTAs). The synthesizer includes a
      voltage-controlled oscillator capable of generating square, triangle, and
      sine waves from 16 Hz to 16 kHz, followed by a state-variable
      voltage-controlled filter, voltage-controlled amplifier, and automatic
      gain control stage.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each major voltage-controlled block can also be modulated using its own
      low-frequency oscillator, operating from 0.1 Hz to 10 Hz, and an ADSR
      envelope generator. Exponential converters are used to shape control
      voltages for musical frequency and amplitude control.
    </p>

    <p>
      The final audio signal is sent through an output amplifier and speaker.
      Alongside the analog circuitry, an ESP32 samples control voltages and audio
      signals and displays their waveforms on an LCD, providing a digital
      visualization of the synthesizer's operation.
    </p>

    <!-- Block Diagram -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Block Diagram</h2>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <img
        src="/synthBlock.png"
        alt="Block diagram of the analog synthesizer"
        class="project-image"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      The block diagram shows the overall architecture of the synthesizer and
      the flow of both audio signals and control voltages. The primary audio path
      begins with waveform generation in the VCO, followed by filtering,
      amplitude control, automatic gain control, and final audio amplification.
      Supporting modulation circuitry, including the low-frequency oscillators,
      ADSR envelope generators, and exponential converters, provides control
      signals to the voltage-controlled stages.
    </p>

    <p>
      The diagram also shows the digital subsystem used to sample and display
      signals on the LCD, as well as the power-management circuitry that supplies
      the analog and digital sections of the system.
    </p>

    <!-- Schematic -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Schematic</h2>

    <div class="border rounded-lg p-6 bg-base-200 text-center">
      <em>Schematic placeholder</em>
    </div>

    <p>
      The schematic contains the complete electrical implementation of the
      synthesizer. It includes the voltage-controlled oscillator, state-variable
      filter, voltage-controlled amplifier, automatic gain control loop, ADSR
      envelope generator, exponential converter, power circuitry, and ESP32
      interface.
    </p>

    <p>
      The major voltage-controlled sections can operate either manually or
      through external control voltages. Potentiometers provide direct control
      over parameters such as oscillator frequency and filter cutoff, while the
      modulation and envelope circuits allow those same parameters to vary
      automatically over time. A separate manual volume control sets the final
      output level before the signal reaches the speaker amplifier.
    </p>

    <!-- Next Steps -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Next Steps</h2>

    <p>
      The next stage of the project is to simulate and verify each subsystem in
      LTSpice against its design targets. Once the individual circuits are
      validated, the complete PCB will be laid out, fabricated, assembled, and
      physically tested. A custom enclosure will then be modeled and 3D printed,
      and the ESP32 firmware responsible for sampling signals and rendering
      waveforms on the LCD will be developed.
    </p>

    <style>
      .project-figure {
        margin: 1.5rem 0;
      }

      .project-image {
        width: 100%;
        display: block;
        border-radius: 8px;
        border: 1px solid #ddd;
        background: #f5f5f5;
      }

      .project-figure figcaption {
        margin-top: 0.5rem;
        text-align: center;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        opacity: 0.7;
      }
    </style>

  </section>

</BaseLayout>